l bet. But since the only discrete option listed besides all-in/fold/check is 100, and other raises must be manually specified, we must pick from valid options.\n\nGiven the choices:  \n- All-in (-2): Overcommitting, poor EV.  \n- Fold (-1): Terrible, strong draw.  \n- Check (0): Keeps optionality, lets opponent bluff or bet a worse hand.  \n- Raise 100: Small, possibly ineffective, but could build pot if behind.  \n\nBut checking maintains balance and control. Given that the opponent called your pre-flop raise, they might have a pair or weaker draw. Letting them bet again on this coordinated board can give you information.\n\nReason:  \nChecking is optimal here because it maintains pot control with a strong draw, avoids overcommitting, and invites bluffs or weaker bets from the opponent on a dangerous (flush-draw-heavy) board. It’s consistent with balanced play in position (assuming you act first, though not explicitly stated). Also, with ~20k stack, preserving chips while chasing a nut-flush draw is prudent.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
