sibly a full house draw that missed\n- Or even top pair thinking they’re good\n\nBut going too high (like 3000+) risks folding out all worse hands.\n\nAlternatively, **checking (0)** could be deceptive — let opponent bluff with a missed draw or medium hand — but given they’ve been passive, unlikely.\n\nThey checked back on multiple streets? No — actually, on flop they bet 100 after your check, then called your 200 — aggressive. Then called 500 on turn.\n\nSo they’re willing to call big bets — likely have *something*.\n\nTherefore, **value betting is correct**.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong King-high flush, which is likely the best hand. Betting around **half to full pot (~1000)** maximizes expected value by getting calls from worse hands while not overfacing the opponent. A raise of **100** is too small — looks weak. Going larger extracts more value.\n\nAnswer: 1000'}
