asoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \n- You hold **Queen of Clubs, 9 of Clubs**.  \n- The board is: **8 of Clubs, Ace of Diamonds, Jack of Spades, 10 of Spades** (four cards revealed — this is the **Turn** round).  \n- There are four cards to a straight: **8, 9, 10, J, Q** — you have all five! Your hand completes a **Straight (8-Q)** using your Q and 9, plus 8, 10, J on the board.  \n- Additionally, you have **two clubs in hand**, and one club on the board (8 of Clubs), giving you **three clubs total** — not enough for a flush yet, but possible draw if another club comes. However, since you already have a straight, that’s your current made hand.  \n- Your opponent called your pre-flop raise, then re-raised, then called your continuation bet on the flop. Their actions suggest strength or drawing interest, but they haven’t shown aggression recently.\n\nYou currently have a **made straight**, which is very strong on this board. It's the highest possible straight (**Ten to Queen**) — no higher straight is possible (King-high straight would require K+Q+J+10+9, but Ace is high, so A-K-Q-J-10 would be needed; we don’t have K). But importantly:  \n- Any player holding **K + X** can complete a **King-high straight (K-Q-J-10-9)** — which beats your Q-high straight.  \n- So while you have a strong hand, it is vulnerable to an opponent holding **King and anything** (especially King + a club or King + Queen).\n\nHowever, given the betting history:\n- You raised pre-flop (200), opponent raised to 400, you called → shows you’re committed.\n- On the flop (which includes 8C, AD, JS), you bet 400 — continuation bet with draw potential (gutshot + flush draw).\n- Opponent called — could mean they have top pair (A-x), middle pair, or a draw themselves.\n\nNow on the **Turn (10♠ added)**, you’ve now hit the straight. This is a great spot to extract value. Since checks have not occurred yet on this street, and you are first to act:\n\nYour valid actions include checking (0), folding (-1), raising at least 100, or going all-in.\n\nBut note: the last bet on the previous round was *your* 400, and opponent called. So the current round has no bets yet — meaning you can check or bet.\n\nBest moves to consider:\n- **Check (0)**: Let opponent bet with their weaker hands/pairs/draws. They might bluff or value bet with worse (e.g., AJ, AQ, AA). This is a reasonable option to induce bluffs.\n- **Raise by 100**: Small bet for thin value, but may not build pot effectively.\n- **Larger raise (e.g., 800–1500)**: Build the pot while representing strength. Given your hand is strong but not unbeatable, you want to get value from worse pairs, two pair, or draws.\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill unless short-stacked. You have ~19,100 chips — too deep to shove now without reason.\n\nOptimal play: **Bet around half to two-thirds of the pot** to extract value. Let's estimate the pot size.\n\nReconstructing pot:\n- Pre-flop: You bet 200 → opponent raised to 400 → you called → total preflop = 800\n- Flop: You bet 400 → opponent called → +800 → total pot now 1600\n- Turn: No new money yet → Pot is 1600\n\nA standard value bet here would be ~800–1000 (50–60%).\n\nGiven your hand is strong but beatable by K-x, you want to charge draws and get value from weaker made hands.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong, nut-like hand (second-nut straight), and the board has both straight and flush possibilities. Betting protects your hand, denies free equity to draws (like Kx chasing a better straight, or someone on a flush draw), and extracts value from weaker holdings like top pair or middle pair. Checking risks giving a free card to complete a King-high straight or a flush.\n\nAnswer should be a **positive number ≥100**, ideally in the range of 800–1200.\n\nSince 100 is available but too small, and larger raises are allowed, choosing **1000** is reasonable for value and protection.\n\nFinal decision:\n\nEvaluation: I have completed a Queen-high straight on the turn, which is strong but vulnerable to King-high straight. The board also has three spades and one club — flush draws possible but not yet completed. Opponent has shown willingness to call with marginal hands. Best line is to bet for value and protection.\nReason: Betting around 1000 builds the pot, extracts value from weaker pairs, and discourages draws. It balances value and risk, avoiding over-committing while maximizing expected gain.\nAnswer: 1000"}
