t if they think they can steal.\n\nBut given you\'ve been betting every round, checking now might signal weakness — especially since Q♠ helps many draws.\n\nAlternatively, bet — to extract value.\n\nMinimum raise is 100, but that’s tiny relative to pot (~3,600).\n\nTo be effective, should bet at least half-pot (~1,800) to full pot (~3,600) or more.\n\nBut "raise" here means increase the bet amount — but in poker, on river, after no bet yet, you can bet.\n\nBut in this system, valid actions include raising by X, where X >= 100.\n\nSince no bet has been made this round yet, a "raise" here functions as a **bet**.\n\nSo raising by 1,800 means you bet 1,800.\n\nOpponent must call 1,800 to continue.\n\nGiven pot is 3,600, that’s a 50% pot bet — reasonable.\n\nCould go bigger — but risk scaring off callers.\n\nAll-in is 18,100 — overkill, might only get called by better hands (flushes), and fold out worse — poor value.\n\nSo best: **size a value bet** to maximize expected value.\n\nPossible actions:\n- Check (0): allow opponent to bluff, but may lose value.\n- Bet small (e.g., 100): too small, almost like check.\n- Bet 1,000–2,000: good value range.\n- Bet large: risk folding out worse.\n\nGiven opponent called your 1,000 raise on the turn, they likely have *something* — maybe top pair, draw, or bluff.\n\nBut after calling three streets, they may have a decent hand.\n\nNow, with Q♠ on river, if they had AQ, they now have two pair (A and Q), which is worse than your straight.\n\nIf they had AJ, they have two pair (A and J), still worse.\n\nIf they had JT, they have two pair (J and 10), worse.\n\nIf they had a spade draw (e.g., K♠9♠), they now have a flush — better than your straight.\n\nSo you beat two pair, trips, etc., but lose to flush.\n\nThus, you want to **get called by worse hands**.\n\nTherefore, **bet for value**.\n\nSize: around 2,000 seems good — about 55% of pot.\n\nRaising by 2,000 is valid (above 100, below 18,100).\n\nAll-in (-2) is too extreme.\n\nFold (-1) 